<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.12.4: Using Graphs to Determine the Domain of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01067</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.12.4: Using Graphs to Determine the Domain of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d75e354a-b2b8-431d-8a3b-2e37ce1ef730</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 Minutes)</title>
<para>This activity is an extension of the expectations in the TEKS.</para>

<title>Launch</title>
<para>Provide access to graphing technology.</para>

<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title><para>Action and Expression: Provide Access for Physical Action</para>
  <para>
      Provide access to tools and assistive technologies such as a graphing calculator or graphing software. Some students may benefit from a checklist or list of steps to be able to use the calculator or software. </para>
      <para><emphasis effect="italics">Supports accessibility for: Organization; Conceptual processing; Attention</emphasis></para></note>



<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>This activity is an extension of the expectations in the TEKS.</para>
<para>Consider the function \(f(x) = \frac{6}{x-2}\).</para>
<para>To find out the sets of possible input and output values of the function, Clare created a table and evaluated \(f\) at some values of \(x\). Along the way, she ran into some trouble.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find \(f(x)\) for each \(x\)-value Clare listed. Describe what Clare’s trouble might be. </item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead/>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
       -10
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(\frac12\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
      2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        8
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis></entry><entry> </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> For example: When \(x\) is \(2\), \(f(x)\) is \(\frac60\), which is undefined. </para>

<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead/>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
       -10
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(\frac12\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
      2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        8
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis></entry><entry>-\(\frac12\)</entry>
      <entry>-3</entry>
      <entry>-4</entry>
      <entry>\(\frac60\)</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use graphing technology to graph function \(f\). What do you notice about the graph? </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> For example: The graph splits where the \(x\)-value is around 2. See graph. 
    <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/96004ae78a7d26533c5df1c35813907286cbdc59"/></media></figure></para>
  <item> Use a calculator to compute the value you and Clare had trouble computing. What do you notice about the computation? </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> When I tried to find \(f(2)\) with a calculator, it gave an error message. </para>
  <item> How would you describe the domain of function \(f\)? </item>

  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> For example: The domain includes all numbers except 2. </para>
</list>
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are You Ready for More?</title>
<para>Why do you think the graph of function \(f\) looks the way it does? Why are there two parts that split at \(x=2\), with one curving down as it approaches \(x=2\) from the left and the other curving up as it approaches \(x=2\) from the right?</para>
<para>Evaluate function \(f\) at different \(x\)-values that approach 2 but are not exactly 2, such as 1.8, 1.9, 1.95, 1.999, 2.2, 2.1, 2.05, 2.001, and so on. What do you notice about the values of \(f(x)\) as the \(x\)-values get closer and closer to 2?</para>
<para>Answer:</para>
<list>
  <item> When \(x\) is less than 2, as it gets closer to 2 (from the left side), the denominator in \(\frac{6}{x-2}\) becomes a smaller and smaller negative value, so the quotient, \(f(x)\), gets larger and larger in the negative direction. It does so very quickly, which explains why the curve bends down rather dramatically. </item>
  <item> When \(x\) is greater than 2, as it approaches 2 (from the right side), the denominator in \(\frac{6}{x-2}\) becomes a smaller and smaller positive value, so \(f(x)\) gets larger and larger in the positive direction. It does so very quickly as well, which explains the graph shooting up if we trace it from right to left. </item>
  <item> The expression \(\frac{6}{x-2}\) is undefined at \(x=2\), and we can see that the two pieces of the graph shoot off in opposite directions as \(x\) approaches 2 from the left and from the right, which explains the gap at \(x=2\). </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Display a graph of the function for all to see. Invite students to share their observations of the behavior of function \(f\) based on the completed table and their graph. Solicit their ideas on what the problem might be with this function.</para>
<para>If no students mentioned division by 0 as the issue, bring this up. Ask questions such as:</para>
<list>
  <item>“What happens when we divide a number by 0?” (The result is undefined.) </item>
  <item>“In the expression \(\frac{6}{x-2}\), what value or values of \(x\) would result in a denominator of 0?” (Only 2 gives a denominator of 0.) </item>
  <item>“If 2 does not produce an output, is it a possible input for \(f\)?” (No) </item>
</list>
<para>Highlight that the domain of \(f\) includes all numbers except 2.</para></section>
</content>
